#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114870 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f61a7c3-82e6-5f90-8a95-4a79078fd542" :knowledge-point/title "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Growth Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "5e2f19a2-f9e3-59dd-83a9-90950b0a55a8" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-20883" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=-2(7^x)-1$." :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=2(7^x)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1682/Source/Images/c0bddb97a59f1d1446397be26bf1e51e.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-2(7^x)$. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,-2)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1682/Source/Images/f447f1ce83ec1094829ff62c4504ced3.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $1$ unit down to give $y=-2(7^x)-1$. The point $(0,-2)$ moves to $(0,-3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1682/Source/Images/6743dca8e28aed3836687c609186c041.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114871 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f4440-ba1a-58ce-9ff2-ccdf591a0d30" :knowledge-point/title "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Decay Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "0ab5addd-b4b1-56b8-8cd2-690d5be81b8a" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-20884" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y = -\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)^x + 2$." :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1682/Source/Images/48b2ab67ce7da1701a79f9ac0149a0aa.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}$. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1682/Source/Images/63dc4b8b2ede638834e47bd348f4e5d9.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $2$ units up to give $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+2$. The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,1)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=2$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1682/Source/Images/f8d53f5d2ab7fb698d8aaf146126ddfd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f4440-ba1a-58ce-9ff2-ccdf591a0d30" :knowledge-point/title "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Decay Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23ed0634-61a3-56d3-a2e3-43d0cc1b951a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932041/assets/779c8384569a3cf6cec79186e69a657b4e23f98f2dd464a3879700b4c08fd27a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244132} {:answer/id #uuid "f340c5a2-bf4a-5f2d-878f-eecd0a554703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932041/assets/cadd20667d93ddc9058703331d2442573b4febc0eb79b0c50749490a859d7006.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244133} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2f15a8-ce03-5eeb-9706-94c63f50594a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932041/assets/b934a5fcadb46619e6c3631e6f9d78c8a0f72beeb023f9696c01422503188586.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244134} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ee7bfb-eac1-5036-98bc-ac3f4c3ccbca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932041/assets/4442c3b03668bf3d4b8090c56c4ad083e85aa4399a0326255fe2322f2dcc729b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244135} {:answer/id #uuid "afa38ae6-be2c-5d3d-886f-4b997425a103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference